  <!-- GLOSSARY TAB -->
  <div class="tab-panel" id="glossary-tab">
    <div class="about-panel">
      <h2>Glossary</h2>
      <p>
        The ERCOT queue is a technical document. A few dozen acronyms and phase names
        show up across the map, filter sidebar, project pages, and API. Here's what they
        mean, in plain English.
      </p>

      <dl class="glossary">
        <dt>◆ AI / data-center flavored</dt>
        <dd>
          A soft classification used on the map and movers page. Flagged when the
          <em>interconnecting entity</em> (developer/owner name) matches a curated list
          of operators clearly connected to the AI/data-center theme —
          <em>CleanAI, LLC</em>, <em>Kalnin Ventures, LLC</em>,
          <em>Palomino Alpha, LLC</em>, <em>Bullock Data Center, LLC</em>,
          <em>Liberty Data Center I, LLC</em> — or when the entity name simply
          contains "Data Center". This is <em>soft evidence, not proof</em>: the
          queue doesn't disclose end-customers. But when developers named "Data
          Center, LLC" file 1.4 GW gas plants, the pattern is worth surfacing.
          Toggle the sidebar checkbox to filter the map to just these.
        </dd>

        <dt>BESS</dt>
        <dd>
          Battery Energy Storage System. Grid-scale batteries. In the ERCOT data they
          show up under fuel code <code>OTH</code> with technology <code>BA</code>.
          They're the dominant category by project count — 881 of the 1,827 projects
          in the current queue.
        </dd>

        <dt>CDR Zone</dt>
        <dd>
          <em>Capacity, Demand and Reserves</em> reporting zone. ERCOT divides Texas
          into six regions — HOUSTON, NORTH, SOUTH, WEST, PANHANDLE, COASTAL — used
          for planning and reserve tracking. Every project is tagged with one.
        </dd>

        <dt>COD</dt>
        <dd>
          <em>Commercial Operation Date.</em> The target date a project expects to
          start generating power for the grid commercially. Slips are the norm —
          126 projects slipped their COD in the July 2026 report alone, totaling
          24 GW of delayed capacity.
        </dd>

        <dt>ERCOT</dt>
        <dd>
          <em>Electric Reliability Council of Texas.</em> The grid operator for most of
          Texas. Publishes the monthly GIS report this site is built on.
        </dd>

        <dt>Fuel code</dt>
        <dd>
          Three-letter code for a project's primary energy source. Common values:
          <code>SOL</code> (solar), <code>WIN</code> (wind), <code>GAS</code> (natural gas),
          <code>OTH</code> (other — mostly batteries), <code>NUC</code> (nuclear),
          <code>WAT</code> (hydro).
        </dd>

        <dt>FIS</dt>
        <dd>
          <em>Full Interconnection Study.</em> The deeper of the two grid-impact studies
          ERCOT performs on a proposed project. Follows the screening study. Can take a
          year or more; when it completes, the project is close to being able to sign
          an Interconnection Agreement.
        </dd>

        <dt>GIM</dt>
        <dd>
          <em>Generation Interconnection or Modification.</em> ERCOT's umbrella term for the
          process of connecting new generation to the grid. The "GIM Study Phase" column
          summarizes where each project is in that process.
        </dd>

        <dt>GIS report</dt>
        <dd>
          <em>Generator Interconnection Status</em> report. The public monthly Excel
          workbook ERCOT publishes at
          <a href="https://www.ercot.com/gridinfo/resource" target="_blank">ercot.com/gridinfo/resource</a>.
          Roughly 1,800 rows, one per proposed project. The raw source for this site.
        </dd>

        <dt>IA</dt>
        <dd>
          <em>Interconnection Agreement.</em> The signed contract between a project and
          ERCOT/the transmission owner that authorizes the project to physically connect
          to the grid. Major late-stage milestone; once signed, the project is likely to
          get built.
        </dd>

        <dt>INR</dt>
        <dd>
          <em>Interconnection Request Number.</em> The unique identifier assigned to
          every project when it enters the queue. Format is
          <code>YYINR####</code> — the first two digits are the year the request was
          filed (e.g. <code>26INR0546</code> was filed in 2026).
        </dd>

        <dt>MW / GW</dt>
        <dd>
          Megawatt / Gigawatt. Units of electrical power (not energy). 1 GW = 1,000 MW.
          A large nuclear reactor is about 1 GW. Texas peak summer demand runs around 85 GW.
          The ERCOT queue totals roughly 440 GW of proposed capacity.
        </dd>

        <dt>POI</dt>
        <dd>
          <em>Point of Interconnection.</em> The specific substation or transmission line
          where a proposed project would physically plug into the grid. Shown on each
          project's detail page.
        </dd>

        <dt>Screening Study (SS)</dt>
        <dd>
          The first, faster study ERCOT performs on a new project — a preliminary
          check for feasibility. Projects that pass move on to the Full Interconnection
          Study.
        </dd>

        <dt>Technology code</dt>
        <dd>
          Two-letter code for how a plant generates electricity. Common values:
          <code>PV</code> (photovoltaic solar), <code>WT</code> (wind turbine),
          <code>CC</code> (combined-cycle gas), <code>GT</code> (gas turbine),
          <code>IC</code> (internal combustion), <code>BA</code> (battery).
        </dd>

        <dt>Withdrawn</dt>
        <dd>
          A project has been removed from the queue — either the developer canceled it
          or ERCOT dropped it for missing milestones. Distinct from a project that
          completes and becomes operational. In the current report, 31 projects were
          withdrawn since the prior month, totaling 5.6 GW.
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ABOUT TAB -->
